            <ArticleTitle>A rare case of idiopathic spontaneous coronary artery dissection causing acute myocardial infarction.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>308-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2459/JCM.0b013e3280117079</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Spontaneous coronary artery dissection is a rare clinical event and often a fatal cause of ischaemic heart disease occurring predominantly in young or middle-aged otherwise healthy patients. We present the case of a 59-year-old female patient who was admitted to our coronary care unit with acute anterior myocardial infarction. Coronary angiography showed a long dissection involving the left coronary artery. Emergency coronary artery bypass grafting was performed unsuccessfully.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
